# LE FORME DELLA PROSA, E CIO' CHE NON SO LEGGERE — gen405.
#
# L'estrattore conosceva UNA forma, «X e' un Y». Misurato sul sogno di
# photosynthesis: sei frasi dense di enciclopedia, UN fatto — `system(...)`,
# vero e quasi vuoto. In prosa enciclopedica quella forma compare una volta,
# nella prima riga; la forma che l'autore usa davvero per dire di che cosa parla
# e' l'elenco con un appiglio.
#
# E la seconda meta', che e' quella che chiude il cerchio col gen404: una frase
# che l'estrattore NON sa leggere e' una lacuna di macchineria, e va nello
# stesso registro dei turni senza ponte. Prima spariva.

[mock live]
!set PARROT0_BASE=kb/core/base.p0
!set PARROT0_SESSION=
!set PARROT0_PROFILE=kb/profiles/agi.p0
!set PARROT0_WORLD_FACTS=1
!set PARROT0_LANG=en
!reset
# gen505y — !timeout dichiarato con la causa (TEST_TODO H.1c): il passaggio ha
# CINQUE frasi e il lettore le legge una per una, ~0,6 s l'una (misurato: 2,9 s).
!timeout 6

# La testa nominale prima della cue, la lista dopo. La testa plurale e' il caso
# normale e va al singolare: «organisms such as…» -> organism(...).
[test an appositive enumeration yields one fact per member]
# gen505y: le pagine curate non esistono piu' (gen436, decisione di F.): si legge
# il passaggio INLINE, che e' anche piu' fedele al ciclo.
> read: Photosynthesis is a system of biological processes by which photopigment-bearing autotrophic organisms, such as most plants, algae and cyanobacteria, convert light energy typically from sunlight into the chemical energy necessary to fuel their metabolism. The term photosynthesis usually refers to oxygenic photosynthesis, a process that releases oxygen as a byproduct of water splitting. Photosynthetic organisms store the converted chemical energy within the bonds of intracellular organic compounds, typically carbohydrates like sugars, starches, phytoglycogen and cellulose. When needing to use this stored energy, an organism's cells then metabolize the organic compounds through cellular respiration. Photosynthesis plays a critical role in producing and maintaining the oxygen content of the Earth's atmosphere, and it supplies most of the biological energy necessary for complex life on Earth.
<~ organism(plants)
!query organism(algae)
!query carbohydrate(cellulose)

# Un solo membro NON e' un'enumerazione: e' una similitudine, e leggerla come
# una classe scrive un fatto falso. E' il cancello che rende usabili cue
# ambigue come «like» e «come».
[test one member is a simile, not a class]
!reset
!assert enumeration_cue("behaves like")
> a fluid behaves like a gas
<! Learned

# La cue e' conoscenza, e la prova e' toglierla: senza «such as» la stessa
# pagina rende molto meno. Nessuna ricompilazione in mezzo.
#
# Il test gira sulla LETTURA di una pagina e non su una frase detta, e il motivo
# va detto invece che nascosto: l'enumerazione e' agganciata solo al percorso
# profondo. Chi DICE «metals such as copper, tin and lead» in conversazione
# riceve ancora un muro, mentre chi la fa LEGGERE viene capito. E' la stessa
# conoscenza per due strade diverse, ed e' un'asimmetria da chiudere.
[test the cue is data, not code]
!reset
# gen505y: le pagine curate non esistono piu' (gen436, decisione di F.): si legge
# il passaggio INLINE, che e' anche piu' fedele al ciclo.
> read: Photosynthesis is a system of biological processes by which photopigment-bearing autotrophic organisms, such as most plants, algae and cyanobacteria, convert light energy typically from sunlight into the chemical energy necessary to fuel their metabolism. The term photosynthesis usually refers to oxygenic photosynthesis, a process that releases oxygen as a byproduct of water splitting. Photosynthetic organisms store the converted chemical energy within the bonds of intracellular organic compounds, typically carbohydrates like sugars, starches, phytoglycogen and cellulose. When needing to use this stored energy, an organism's cells then metabolize the organic compounds through cellular respiration. Photosynthesis plays a critical role in producing and maintaining the oxygen content of the Earth's atmosphere, and it supplies most of the biological energy necessary for complex life on Earth.
<~ carbohydrate(cellulose)
!reset
!forget enumeration_cue("such as")
!forget enumeration_cue("like")
# gen505y: le pagine curate non esistono piu' (gen436, decisione di F.): si legge
# il passaggio INLINE, che e' anche piu' fedele al ciclo.
> read: Photosynthesis is a system of biological processes by which photopigment-bearing autotrophic organisms, such as most plants, algae and cyanobacteria, convert light energy typically from sunlight into the chemical energy necessary to fuel their metabolism. The term photosynthesis usually refers to oxygenic photosynthesis, a process that releases oxygen as a byproduct of water splitting. Photosynthetic organisms store the converted chemical energy within the bonds of intracellular organic compounds, typically carbohydrates like sugars, starches, phytoglycogen and cellulose. When needing to use this stored energy, an organism's cells then metabolize the organic compounds through cellular respiration. Photosynthesis plays a critical role in producing and maintaining the oxygen content of the Earth's atmosphere, and it supplies most of the biological energy necessary for complex life on Earth.
<! carbohydrate(cellulose)

# La seconda meta': cio' che non ha saputo leggere lo sa dire.
[test unreadable prose becomes a machinery gap]
!reset
!query! machinery_gap($X)
# gen505y: le pagine curate non esistono piu' (gen436, decisione di F.): si legge
# il passaggio INLINE, che e' anche piu' fedele al ciclo.
> read: Photosynthesis is a system of biological processes by which photopigment-bearing autotrophic organisms, such as most plants, algae and cyanobacteria, convert light energy typically from sunlight into the chemical energy necessary to fuel their metabolism. The term photosynthesis usually refers to oxygenic photosynthesis, a process that releases oxygen as a byproduct of water splitting. Photosynthetic organisms store the converted chemical energy within the bonds of intracellular organic compounds, typically carbohydrates like sugars, starches, phytoglycogen and cellulose. When needing to use this stored energy, an organism's cells then metabolize the organic compounds through cellular respiration. Photosynthesis plays a critical role in producing and maintaining the oxygen content of the Earth's atmosphere, and it supplies most of the biological energy necessary for complex life on Earth.
<~ extracted
!query machinery_gap($X)
> what did you fail on
<~ could not bridge
